<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lithuanians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lithuanians are a Baltic people living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, on the eastern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, who speak Lithuanian, one of the two surviving Baltic languages. Their tribes were united in the 13th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> became one of the largest states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. They were among the last Europeans to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, in 1387, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>'s union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lithuanians.jpg" alt="Lithuanians" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allice Hunter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Lithuanian_Diaspora_in_the_World.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Theories about the origin of the Lithuanians have been recorded since the 15th century, when Lithuanian nobles, like those of many European nations, sought a noble and ancient descent. The most influential was the Palemon theory, which held that Lithuanians descended from Roman patricians led by a nobleman named Palemon who fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Modern scholarship holds that the Lithuanians formed from eastern Baltic tribes.</p><p>The Lithuanian nation traces its origins to the Lithuanian tribe and the land called Litua in the Annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quedlinburg">Quedlinburg</a> of 1009. In Rus chronicles of the 11th and 12th centuries it appears as Litva. Over the centuries, especially under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, neighbouring tribes consolidated into the Lithuanian nation, mainly as a defence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-slavs">Eastern Slavs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Historians date the formation of the Lithuanian state variously between the 11th and the mid-13th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s only crowned king and its first baptised ruler, is generally considered its founder. The Lithuanians were the only branch of the Baltic peoples to create a state before the modern era. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-crusade">Lithuanian Crusade</a> ravaged the country in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and ended only with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-melno">Treaty of Melno</a> in 1422, so it persisted after the Christianization of Lithuania in 1387, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s last pagan people were baptised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> was baptised in 1413.</p><p>The state reached its height under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas the Great</a> (r. 1392–1430), when it ruled the lands between the Baltic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569 the Grand Duchy kept its independence within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> until 1795.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Lithuanians distinguish five historic regional groups: the Aukštaičiai, Dzūkai, Žemaičiai or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitians">Samogitians</a>, Suvalkiečiai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lietuvininkai">Lietuvininkai</a>, the last virtually extinct. The Žemaičiai of the west speak a dialect so distinctive that it is sometimes considered a separate language. The Lietuvininkai lived in Lithuania Minor, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>, were mostly Lutheran, and published the first printed work in Lithuanian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martynas-mazvydas">Martynas Mažvydas</a>'s Catechism of 1547; most were expelled or fled after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>.</p><p>Lithuanian is conservative in grammar and phonology, keeping archaic features otherwise found in ancient languages such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedic-sanskrit">Vedic Sanskrit</a> and Ancient Greek, and is an important source for reconstructing Proto-Indo-European. A ban on printing Lithuanian was lifted in 1904. Most Lithuanians belong to the Catholic Church. Traditional life was rural, with mixed farming and forest foraging. The summer solstice holiday Joninės has pagan origins.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lithuanians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its population was mostly Poles, Polish Jews, ethnic Germans, Ukrainians and <b>Lithuanians</b>, with a small Russian minority, and mainly Roman Catholic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Pressure from the crusading orders encouraged unification: in 1236 a force of Samogitians and other <b>Lithuanians</b> annihilated the Livonian Brothers of the Sword at the Battle of Saule, the event from which the state is conventionally dated here.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>To avoid being incorporated into the German Empire, <b>Lithuanians</b> elected the Monaco-born Mindaugas II titular King of Lithuania in July 1918; he never assumed the throne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1237 the Order also absorbed the remnants of the Livonian Brothers of the Sword, shattered by the <b>Lithuanians</b> at the Battle of Saule, and so gained a northern branch in Livonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A small group of Curonian freemen, the 'Curonian kings', kept a special status into modern times, and the people merged into the Latvians and <b>Lithuanians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/curonians">Curonians</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy suffered raids by the <b>Lithuanians</b> and the Teutonic Order.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-leczyca">Duchy of Leczyca</a></li></ul></section>
